@startuml proto-freqmeter-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-freqmeter-timing.puml - (c) the input edge timing, and the accuracy
' the instrument actually has.
'
' Measured by tb/tb_pe_soc_freqmeter.v on real RTL (60 MHz, Icarus, the real
' SRAM macro) with firmware/freqmeter.pe. Twelve banked points, 158 Hz to
' 10 000 Hz, each checked against a SECOND, INDEPENDENT measurement of the pad
' rather than against the generator's table.
' ===========================================================================

title Frequency + duty meter - edge timing and the accuracy it has\n<font size=12>12 points, 158 Hz to 10 000 Hz; three are one tick out and are printed so</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "GENERATOR\nedge-driven, absolute\ndelays" as GEN
participant "the pad\npin 6" as PAD
participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v" as CPU
participant "the 1 us counter\nport 4, I2CTICK" as TICK
participant "dmem[0..3]\nT and H" as ACC

== a period arrives from outside ==

GEN -> PAD : the line goes HIGH
note over GEN
  <b>THE GENERATOR IS EDGE-DRIVEN WITH ABSOLUTE DELAYS</b>,
  not woken once per clock. A 2.6 M clock testbench that
  woke up 2.6 M times to decide whether the pin had moved
  would spend its wall time deciding it had not.
end note
CPU -> TICK : read the COUNTER, not the flag
note over TICK
  <b>60 clocks = exactly 1 us.</b> Port 0x4.

  <b>NOT port 0x7 (STATUS), WHICH IS 260 CLOCKS = 4.33 us</b> -
  the UART half-bit tick, which exists to place a serial
  sample mid-cell. The first version counted it and
  measured <b>0.24 ticks per microsecond</b>, reporting a
  200 us period as 46. A tick counter whose name says
  TIMER is not a microsecond.
end note
TICK -> CPU : now
CPU -> CPU : elapsed = (now - dmem[7]) mod 256
note over CPU
  <b>THERE IS NO FLAG TO LOSE, AND THAT IS WHY THE
  COUNTER IS READ RATHER THAN THE FLAG.</b>

  A clear-on-read flag is ONE BIT, so a poll loop that takes
  longer than a tick reports several ticks as one, and the
  loss is invisible in the result - the measurement is
  simply short, by an amount that depends on how busy the
  program was. This program is one instruction from that
  defect and does not have it.

  Exact for any loop up to 255 us, and this loop is
  <b>1.3 us</b> - two hundred times inside it.
end note
CPU -> ACC : T += elapsed (16-bit add)

== the rising edge: the design decision ==

CPU -> CPU : dmem[4] != 0x40 and the pad is high
CPU -> ACC : T = 0, H = 0 - BOTH counters reset
note over ACC
  <b>THE PERIOD IS T AT THE RISING EDGE, BECAUSE T IS RESET
  AT EVERY RISING EDGE. THE HIGH TIME IS H AT THE RISING
  EDGE, BECAUSE H IS ALSO RESET THERE AND STOPS COUNTING
  AT THE FALLING EDGE.</b>
  ...
  <b>TWO RESETS REMOVE EVERY 16-BIT SUBTRACT, EVERY COMPARE
  AND EVERY DIVIDE.</b> And this machine has no carry flag,
  so a 16-bit subtract is a four-case analysis on bit 7 and
  a compare is the same analysis again. There is none of
  either in the program, and that is the design rather than a
  limitation worked around.

  The commit message called this act "capture only" before a
  line of it existed, and that is what it turned out to be,
  for a reason the commit message could not have known.
end note

== the high time: the falling edge only STOPS the counter ==

GEN -> PAD : the line goes LOW
CPU -> ACC : H stops counting here
note over ACC
  Nothing to subtract. The accumulator simply stops, and at
  the NEXT rising edge whatever is in H is the high time of
  the period that just ended.
end note

== the 16-bit add, and the guard it does not need ==

note over CPU
  <b>THREE ARITHMETIC FACTS DECIDED THE WHOLE PROGRAM.</b>
  ...
  1. <b>A 16-bit INCREMENT IS CHEAP.</b> +1 on the low byte is exactly
     zero when it carries, so the carry test is one JZ on the
     low result.
  ...
  2. <b>A 16-BIT ADD OF A VARIABLE IS AFFORDABLE, BUT ONLY JUST.</b>
     The carry test is the same JZ, except that 0 + 0 = 0 is
     not a carry, so a general addend needs a GUARD: when the
     low result is zero, reload the low operand and test it
     for zero too.
  ...
     <b>This program gets that guard away FOR FREE,</b> because its
     addend is the elapsed microsecond count and is therefore
     NEVER ZERO - "the low byte came out zero" can then only
     mean "the low byte wrapped". The guard is not skipped, it
     is UNNECESSARY, and the comment says which. A program
     adding two memory values would need it.
  ...
  3. <b>A 16-BIT SUBTRACT IS A DIFFERENT MACHINE,</b> and point 3 is
     why this program contains none.
end note

== the bank, every third period ==

CPU -> CPU : every 3 periods, bank (T, H) into the slot dmem[5] names
note over ACC
  <b>THE FIRST PERIOD OF EACH RUN IS A WARM-UP AND IS NOT
  CHECKED</b>, for a reason that is a property of the PROTOCOL
  and not of the firmware: the firmware starts in the middle
  of it, so the time since the previous rising edge is not a
  period.

  <b>A receiver that starts mid-period misses that one period,</b>
  and a testbench that pretended otherwise would be asking the
  firmware to measure something it was never present for.
end note
CPU -> ACC : T high H low into dmem[slot*2 .. slot*2+1]
note over ACC
  <b>dmem[5] DOUBLES AS THE "NOT STARTED YET" STATE (FF),</b>
  because the program needed one byte more than the machine
  has and the slot index is the one value that is not a
  measurement. The map uses all sixteen bytes and one byte is
  shared rather than found.
end note

== the accuracy the instrument actually has ==

note over GEN, ACC
  <b>Every banked point, checked against a SECOND, INDEPENDENT
  measurement of the pad</b> - the receiver in the TB, not
  the generator's table. A generator that knew the answer
  would agree with a firmware that had the sweep wrong.
  ...
  | point | period | high | duty | [pad] |
  |  0 |   8 000 | 6 000 | 75.0 % | [8000.000 / 75.00 %] |
  |  1 | <b>10 001</b> | 2 500 | 25.0 % | [10000.000 / 25.00 %] |
  |  2 |   3 150 | 1 039 | 33.0 % | [3150.000 / 33.00 %] |
  |  3 |   4 000 |   400 | 10.0 % | [4000.000 / 10.00 %] |
  |  4 |   1 600 | 1 056 | 66.0 % | [1600.000 / 66.00 %] |
  |  5 |   2 000 | 1 600 | 80.0 % | [2000.000 / 80.00 %] |
  |  6 | <b>    801</b> |   480 | 59.9 % | [800.000 / 60.00 %] |
  |  7 |   1 000 |   399 | 39.9 % | [1000.000 / 40.00 %] |
  |  8 |     400 |   180 | 45.0 % | [400.000 / 45.00 %] |
  |  9 |     500 |   275 | 55.0 % | [500.000 / 55.00 %] |
  | 10 |      80 |    12 | 15.0 % | [80.000 / 15.00 %] |
  | 11 |     100 |    50 | 50.0 % | [100.000 / 50.00 %] |
  ...
  <b>EVERY PERIOD IS EXACT EXCEPT THREE, BY EXACTLY ONE TICK</b> - 10 001,
  801 and 399 against 10 000, 800 and 400 - and the duty follows to a tenth
  of a point because the two counts share the same edge discipline.
  ...
  <b>THAT IS THE RESOLUTION SHOWING UP IN THE LOG INSTEAD OF HIDING IN A
  TOLERANCE,</b> which is what a measured-truth record should look like.
end note

note over GEN, ACC
  <b>WHY THE TOLERANCE IS PER POINT AND NOT ONE NUMBER.</b>
  The instrument's resolution IS the specification:
  ...
  | point | period | relative error of a 1 us counter |
  | 10 000 us (100 Hz) | 10 000 | <b>0.01 %</b> |
  |      100 us (10 kHz) |    100 | <b>1.0 %</b> |
  ...
  So the tolerance is 1 % of the period with a floor of 1.5 ticks. At the fast
  end of the sweep the TOLERANCE is the dominant term and the quantisation is a
  percent of it. <b>A single accuracy number would be a false claim at one end or
  the other,</b> and that is why the act states per point.
  ...
  <b>The 16-bit claim is point 1: 10 001 us, from a counter whose low byte wrapped
  39 TIMES.</b> A firmware counting into a byte reports 16 us there, and has no
  symptom at any other point in the sweep.
end note

@enduml
